# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is gitignored; this file is not, so never
# put a real credential here.
#
# Read by the signaling server at startup and handed to both peers in
# `registered` and `joined` — see server/src/ice.ts. Neither the renter page nor
# the host .exe has to be rebuilt to change any of it.

# Port the signaling server listens on. Defaults to 8080.
PORT=8080

# The platform's Postgres database: Neon's connection string works as given,
# e.g. postgresql://user:password@host/dbname?sslmode=require. The server makes
# its tables on start. Unset, it keeps everything in memory and loses it on
# restart. `npm run seed-requirements` fills its game_requirements table and
# needs it set.
DATABASE_URL=

# Caps on open renter event streams (GET /api/events): server-wide and per
# signed-in renter. Defaults 500 and 10; extra streams are refused with 429.
MAX_EVENT_STREAMS=
MAX_EVENT_STREAMS_PER_RENTER=

# --- Room access --------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Required. With either one missing the server refuses every gaming PC or every
# renter — there is no open mode. See server/src/access.ts.
#
# Signs renters' join tickets. At least 32 characters; make one with
#   openssl rand -base64 48
# Changing it invalidates every ticket already handed out.
ROOM_SECRET=

# One entry per gaming PC, `machine-id:sha256-of-its-key:owner-steam-id`,
# comma-separated. The owner's 17-digit Steam id keeps them from ever being
# matched to their own PC; an entry without one can be matched to anyone.
# `npm run machine-key -- <machine-id> <owner-steam-id>` prints the key (for the
# host app) and the entry to paste here. Only the hash lives on the server.
MACHINE_KEYS=

# Join links for renters: `npm run ticket -- <machine-id> [minutes] [origin]`.

# Which credential may serve a renter — see server/src/attestation.ts.
#   optional  (default) the machine key hosts too, unattested: development only.
#   required  only a host certificate from attestation hosts; the machine key
#             keeps availability, heartbeat and ending a session.
# Anything else counts as required.
HOSTING_ATTESTATION=

# Who judges a machine's attestation. Unset: nobody, so no machine can attest.
#   tpm           the production verifier (server/src/tpm-verifier.ts): the
#                 machine's TPM proves it booted an untouched, Swiff-signed
#                 Swiff OS. Needs ROOM_SECRET and the three settings below;
#                 any of them missing leaves no verifier, with a startup warning.
#   insecure-dev  believes whatever the holder of the machine's own key claims
#                 (evidence { machineKey, facts }): for VMs and tests only,
#                 never where renters play.
ATTESTATION_VERIFIER=

# For ATTESTATION_VERIFIER=tpm. A directory of TPM vendor certificates (roots and
# intermediates, PEM or DER) in two subdirectories: firmware/ for firmware TPMs
# (Intel PTT, AMD fTPM, Microsoft Pluton) and discrete/ for TPM chips (Infineon,
# STMicro, Nuvoton), which host at the lower tier. Microsoft's TrustedTpm.cab is
# one source of them. e.g. /etc/swiff/tpm-roots
ATTESTATION_TPM_ROOTS=

# For ATTESTATION_VERIFIER=tpm. The signed boot policy: which Swiff OS releases
# may host. Each release lists its golden PCRs 11, 12 and 13, its boot
# applications, its UKIs and its Secure Boot authorities (PCR 7); a policy
# missing any of them is refused — see server/src/boot-policy.ts.
# Sign one with `npm run boot-policy -- <payload.json> <private-key.pem>`.
# e.g. /etc/swiff/boot-policy.json
ATTESTATION_POLICY=

# For ATTESTATION_VERIFIER=tpm. The PEM public key the boot policy is signed
# with (Ed25519, RSA or ECDSA). e.g. /etc/swiff/boot-policy.pub.pem
ATTESTATION_POLICY_KEY=

# --- Renter sign-in -------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Required to sign in, and so to book. Signs the renter's sign-in session cookie
# after Steam sign-in — see server/src/signin.ts. At least 32 characters and not
# the same as ROOM_SECRET; make one with
#   openssl rand -base64 48
# Changing it signs every renter out.
SESSION_SECRET=

# Optional. A Steam Web API key (https://steamcommunity.com/dev/apikey) that
# reads a signed-in renter's Steam name, avatar and game library — see
# server/src/steam.ts. Sign-in works without it, but the profile and library
# stay empty: the renter shows as signed in with no name, and the wall offers
# only free-to-play games.
STEAM_API_KEY=

# --- TURN -------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Only needed when the two peers cannot reach each other directly: symmetric
# NAT, carrier CGNAT (a phone hotspot is the usual way to hit this), or a
# network that only lets 443 out. On one LAN leave all of it blank — no TURN is
# configured and the server behaves exactly as it does without this file.
#
# The server mints a credential for each renter's seat when they join, one for
# the renter and one for the PC, each good only until the session ends
# (server/src/ice.ts). Neither side is ever given the relay's own secret. Set
# the relay's URLs and one way to mint; a relay set up wrong runs no relay and
# says why at start.

#    Comma-separated. Include the TCP/443 entries, not just UDP: they are the
#    ones that work on networks where everything but HTTPS is blocked.
TURN_URLS=

# 1. SELF-RUN — coturn with `use-auth-secret` and `static-auth-secret=<this>`
#    (the TURN REST API). The server mints credentials itself, with no network
#    call; the relay refuses each once its session has ended. 32 characters or
#    more, and it belongs here and in the relay's config, nowhere else.
TURN_SECRET=

# 2. BOUGHT — an endpoint that mints credentials, POSTed {"ttl": <seconds>}
#    with the token as a bearer header, answering {"iceServers": ...}. For
#    Cloudflare Realtime TURN (dash.cloudflare.com -> Realtime -> TURN), the
#    TURN key's id and API token:
#
#      TURN_CREDENTIAL_URL=https://rtc.live.cloudflare.com/v1/turn/keys/<key id>/credentials/generate-ice-servers
#      TURN_CREDENTIAL_TOKEN=<the key's API token>
#
#    The token can mint unlimited credentials against the account: not in a
#    commit, not pasted into a chat. With these, TURN_URLS may stay blank: the
#    endpoint's own URLs are used (but port 53, which browsers do not dial).
TURN_CREDENTIAL_URL=
TURN_CREDENTIAL_TOKEN=

# --- A note on cost ----------------------------------------------------------
#
# Relay is metered. At DEFAULT_CAPTURE.maxBitrate (10 Mbit/s) a session burns
# roughly 4.5 GB an hour, so turn that down while testing. Direct connections
# (`host` or `srflx` in the status line) cost nothing — TURN is only the
# fallback when there is no direct path.
